# Project a Thread

## Overview

Your support Agent's Thread holds every message, failed request, and runtime
record it has ever seen. The model must see only the part that forms a valid,
bounded conversation. `Jido.AI.Thread.Projection` makes that selection and
records why each message was kept or dropped:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, projection} = Projection.context(session, %{max_messages: 64})

Enum.map(projection.decisions, &{&1.seq, &1.state, &1.reason})
# => [
#      {0, :included, :projected},
#      {2, :included, :projected},
#      {3, :included, :projected},
#      {4, :included, :projected},
#      {6, :excluded, :request_not_completed},
#      {8, :excluded, :incomplete_tool_exchange},
#      {9, :included, :projected}
#    ]
```

You'll build one support-desk Thread by hand, project it, and read each
decision. No model call runs in this notebook.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

## Start a Session

An AI Agent keeps its conversation in `state.session`. Here you create the
same value directly, with fixed timestamps so the results repeat:

```elixir
alias Jido.AI.Thread.Projection

session = Jido.Session.new(id: "session:support-1", now: 1_000)

{session.rev, Jido.Thread.entry_count(session.thread)}
```

The result is `{0, 0}`.

## Record a Completed Turn

`Projection.append/4` stores one model message as an `:ai_message` entry.
`Projection.observe/5` stores any other record. The refs tie entries to the
request that wrote them. This turn asks about an order, calls
`lookup_order`, and answers:

```elixir
run_1 = %{request_id: "req-1", run_id: "run-1"}

{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(session, %{role: :user, content: "Where is order A-1001?"}, run_1, 1_001)

session = Projection.observe(session, :ai_model_attempt, %{status: :ok}, run_1, 1_002)

{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(
    session,
    %{
      role: :assistant,
      content: "",
      tool_calls: [%{id: "call-1", name: "lookup_order", arguments: %{order_id: "A-1001"}}]
    },
    run_1,
    1_003
  )

{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(
    session,
    %{
      role: :tool,
      name: "lookup_order",
      tool_call_id: "call-1",
      content: ~s({"eta":"2026-11-02","order_id":"A-1001","status":"shipped"})
    },
    run_1,
    1_004
  )

{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(
    session,
    %{role: :assistant, content: "Order A-1001 shipped. It arrives on 2026-11-02."},
    run_1,
    1_005
  )

session = Projection.observe(session, :ai_request_outcome, %{status: :completed}, run_1, 1_006)

Enum.map(session.thread.entries, &{&1.seq, &1.kind})
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {0, :ai_message},
  {1, :ai_model_attempt},
  {2, :ai_message},
  {3, :ai_message},
  {4, :ai_message},
  {5, :ai_request_outcome}
]
```

The Thread assigns `seq` from 0. Only `:ai_message` entries can reach the
model.

## Add a Failed Request

A second request asks for a refund and fails. Its user message stays in the
Thread for inspection. The `:ai_request_outcome` entry with
`status: :failed` marks every message with `run_id: "run-2"` as ineligible:

```elixir
run_2 = %{request_id: "req-2", run_id: "run-2"}

{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(session, %{role: :user, content: "Refund order A-1001."}, run_2, 1_010)

session = Projection.observe(session, :ai_request_outcome, %{status: :failed}, run_2, 1_011)

session.rev
```

The result is `8`.

## Add an Open Tool Call

An assistant message that asks for `issue_refund` but never gets a matching
tool result is an incomplete exchange. Sending it would make the provider
reject the request:

```elixir
{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(
    session,
    %{
      role: :assistant,
      content: "",
      tool_calls: [
        %{id: "call-2", name: "issue_refund", arguments: %{order_id: "A-1001", amount_cents: 2_500}}
      ]
    },
    %{},
    1_020
  )

{:ok, session} =
  Projection.append(session, %{role: :user, content: "When will it arrive?"}, %{}, 1_021)

Jido.Thread.entry_count(session.thread)
```

The result is `10`.

## Project the Thread

`Projection.context/2` returns the canonical `ReqLLM.Context` and the
decisions behind it:

```elixir
{:ok, projection} = Projection.context(session, %{max_messages: 64, max_bytes: 200_000})

Enum.map(projection.context.messages, & &1.role)
```

The result is `[:user, :assistant, :tool, :assistant, :user]`. The failed
refund request and the open `issue_refund` call are gone.

```elixir
Enum.map(projection.decisions, &{&1.seq, &1.state, &1.reason})
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  {0, :included, :projected},
  {2, :included, :projected},
  {3, :included, :projected},
  {4, :included, :projected},
  {6, :excluded, :request_not_completed},
  {8, :excluded, :incomplete_tool_exchange},
  {9, :included, :projected}
]
```

There is one decision per `:ai_message` entry. Observations at seq 1, 5, and
7 never become candidates.

| Reason | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `:projected` | Sent to the model. |
| `:request_not_completed` | Its `run_id` has an `:ai_request_outcome` whose status isn't `:completed`. |
| `:incomplete_tool_exchange` | An assistant tool call without a result for every call ID, or a tool result without its call. |
| `:outside_context_window` | Valid, but older than the limits allow. |
| `:not_eligible` | Excluded for another reason. |

Messages with no `run_id`, or whose run has no outcome yet, are eligible.
That is how the current request's user message reaches the model before
its outcome exists.

## Read the Counts

```elixir
Map.take(projection, [
  :available_messages,
  :included_messages,
  :dropped_messages,
  :first_seq,
  :last_seq
])
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  available_messages: 5,
  included_messages: 5,
  dropped_messages: 0,
  first_seq: 0,
  last_seq: 9
}
```

`available_messages` counts messages that passed eligibility and the
tool-exchange check. `dropped_messages` counts only messages cut by the
limits.

## Apply Limits

Limits drop whole turns, oldest first. A turn starts at a user message and
runs until the next one, so a tool call never loses its result:

```elixir
{:ok, bounded} = Projection.context(session, %{max_messages: 2})

{Enum.map(bounded.context.messages, & &1.role), bounded.dropped_messages}
```

The result is `{[:user], 4}`. The first turn has four messages, so it no
longer fits beside the newest turn.

```elixir
bounded.decisions
|> Enum.filter(&(&1.reason == :outside_context_window))
|> Enum.map(& &1.seq)
```

The result is `[0, 2, 3, 4]`.

| Key | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `max_messages` | Most messages to include. |
| `max_bytes` | Most bytes of JSON-encoded messages to include. |

A missing or non-positive limit means no limit. The newest turn is always
included, even when it alone exceeds a limit. The ReAct Flow passes its
`context` input here, with defaults `max_messages: 64` and
`max_bytes: 200_000`.

## Trace a Message

Each projected `ReqLLM.Message` carries its Thread source in
`metadata.jido.source`:

```elixir
[_user, _call, tool_result | _rest] = projection.context.messages

Map.take(tool_result.metadata.jido.source, [:type, :seq, :refs])
```

The result is
`%{type: :thread_entry, seq: 3, refs: %{request_id: "req-1", run_id: "run-1"}}`.

The tool call keeps its ID and decoded arguments:

```elixir
[_user, call | _rest] = projection.context.messages
[tool_call] = call.tool_calls

{tool_call.id, tool_call.function.name, tool_call.function.arguments}
```

The result is `{"call-1", "lookup_order", ~s({"order_id":"A-1001"})}`.

## Other Projections

`Projection.messages/1` returns portable message maps with no limits:

```elixir
{:ok, messages} = Projection.messages(session)

Enum.map(messages, &{&1.role, &1.content})
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  user: "Where is order A-1001?",
  assistant: "",
  tool: ~s({"eta":"2026-11-02","order_id":"A-1001","status":"shipped"}),
  assistant: "Order A-1001 shipped. It arrives on 2026-11-02.",
  user: "When will it arrive?"
]
```

`Projection.breakpoint/1` returns the last sequence a projection can
include:

```elixir
Projection.breakpoint(session)
```

The result is `9`.

| Function | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `Projection.context/2` | `ReqLLM.Context`, decisions, counts, and portable entries |
| `Projection.explain/2` | Decisions, counts, and portable entries without a `ReqLLM.Context` |
| `Projection.project/2` | Message maps and counts |
| `Projection.messages/1` | Message maps with no limits |
| `Projection.breakpoint/1` | The last includable `seq`, or `nil` |

Each returns `{:error, :invalid_context}` for a Thread that fails
validation or a stored message it can't decode.

## Summary

- Only `:ai_message` entries can reach the model. Observations stay in the
  Thread.
- A request outcome other than `:completed` removes every message from that
  run.
- Tool calls reach the model only with a result for every call ID.
- `max_messages` and `max_bytes` drop whole turns, oldest first, and always
  keep the newest turn.
- Every message maps back to its Thread entry through
  `metadata.jido.source`.

